1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,194
At the Corner Cafe, Asha's staff quiz from
Chapter 20 works well.

2
00:00:05,214 --> 00:00:06,950
Lina scores 4 out of 4.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,467
Then the app closes.

4
00:00:08,487 --> 00:00:13,077
The next morning, Asha runs it again, and
every score is gone.

5
00:00:13,687 --> 00:00:16,401
This is Code Quest, Chapter 21.

6
00:00:16,421 --> 00:00:19,047
Today, data learns to survive.

7
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:23,442
And an AI assistant will promise a
function that keeps every score.

8
00:00:23,462 --> 00:00:25,411
It will sound completely sure.

9
00:00:25,431 --> 00:00:27,576
You will check the file yourself.

10
00:00:28,187 --> 00:00:31,540
Here is today's mission: Asha's score
board.

11
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:34,837
Lines 7 and 8 save the new score to a
file.

12
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:38,787
Lines 11 to 16 load every score from
earlier rounds.

13
00:00:38,807 --> 00:00:41,137
Let's build it, piece by piece.

14
00:00:41,717 --> 00:00:43,897
Write a text file, and read it back.

15
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:47,193
Choose the right mode: write, add or read.

16
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:49,033
Read a file line by line.

17
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:52,337
Save and load tables as CSV files.

18
00:00:52,367 --> 00:00:55,880
And fix the common file errors, with an AI
assistant.

19
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:58,037
Why did the scores vanish?

20
00:00:58,057 --> 00:01:02,919
Variables live in the computer's memory,
and memory is cleared when the program

21
00:01:02,939 --> 00:01:03,290
stops.

22
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:06,625
A file is data saved on the disk, with a
name.

23
00:01:06,645 --> 00:01:09,793
It stays there, so the next run can load
it.

24
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:14,308
Saved games, app settings, a shop's sales:
they are all files.

25
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:17,888
A program talks to a file in three steps.

26
00:01:17,908 --> 00:01:18,921
Open the file.

27
00:01:18,941 --> 00:01:21,060
Read from it, or write to it.

28
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:22,093
Then close it.

29
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:24,626
The memory side is lost at the end.

30
00:01:24,646 --> 00:01:26,061
The disk side stays.

31
00:01:26,653 --> 00:01:29,833
When you open a file, the mode says what
you want to do.

32
00:01:29,867 --> 00:01:30,445
R reads.

33
00:01:30,465 --> 00:01:34,257
It is the default, and the file must
already exist.

34
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:34,962
W writes.

35
00:01:34,982 --> 00:01:39,660
It creates the file, or empties it first
if it already exists.

36
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:40,333
A appends.

37
00:01:40,353 --> 00:01:44,119
It adds to the end, and creates the file
if it is missing.

38
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:46,819
Think of the cafe's order book.

39
00:01:46,839 --> 00:01:51,330
You can read it, start a new empty book,
or add a line at the end.

40
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:55,499
But a person would never tear out every
page by accident.

41
00:01:55,519 --> 00:01:58,413
Mode W empties the file with no warning.

42
00:01:59,020 --> 00:01:59,996
One more idea.

43
00:02:00,016 --> 00:02:02,345
A file stores bytes, not letters.

44
00:02:02,365 --> 00:02:06,900
An encoding is the rule that turns letters
into bytes, and back.

45
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:08,521
UTF-8 is the standard.

46
00:02:08,541 --> 00:02:13,269
It stores every language, including the e
with an accent in Café.

47
00:02:13,289 --> 00:02:18,529
Older versions of Python can use a
different encoding on some computers,

48
00:02:18,549 --> 00:02:19,844
mostly on Windows.

49
00:02:19,864 --> 00:02:22,620
So always write encoding equals utf 8.

50
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:23,979
Quick check.

51
00:02:23,999 --> 00:02:27,030
Asha wants to add a line and keep the old
ones.

52
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:27,744
Which mode?

53
00:02:39,113 --> 00:02:39,268
B.

54
00:02:39,288 --> 00:02:39,881
Append.

55
00:02:39,901 --> 00:02:42,419
W would empty the file first.

56
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:43,600
Breather.

57
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:45,267
Three steps, three modes.

58
00:02:45,287 --> 00:02:46,800
That is the whole idea.

59
00:02:46,833 --> 00:02:49,306
Next, Asha writes her first file.

60
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:54,577
Step 1.

61
00:02:54,597 --> 00:02:56,687
You met Path in Chapter 18.

62
00:02:56,707 --> 00:03:00,830
Line 4 makes a path for a file called note
dot t x t.

63
00:03:00,867 --> 00:03:04,112
Line 5 writes the whole text into the
file.

64
00:03:04,132 --> 00:03:07,377
The text ends with backslash n, a new
line.

65
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,780
Line 6 reads the whole file back as one
string, and prints it.

66
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:15,341
Line 7 asks: does this file exist now?

67
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:27,147
True.

68
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:29,323
Line 5 created the file.

69
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:30,856
And see the empty line?

70
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:35,186
The text already ends with a new line, and
print adds another one.

71
00:03:35,783 --> 00:03:36,216
Step 2.

72
00:03:36,236 --> 00:03:41,136
The with statement opens a file, and
closes it again when the indented block

73
00:03:41,156 --> 00:03:43,143
ends, even if an error happens.

74
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:46,401
Lines 2 to 4 use mode W, and write two
lines.

75
00:03:46,421 --> 00:03:51,897
Write does not add a new line by itself,
so each text ends with backslash n.

76
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:55,533
Lines 6 and 7 use mode A, and write Lina.

77
00:03:55,567 --> 00:03:58,133
Line 9 has no mode, so it reads.

78
00:04:09,513 --> 00:04:10,806
All three names.

79
00:04:10,826 --> 00:04:14,253
Mode A added Lina, and kept Asha and Omar.

80
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:19,651
And end equals empty, from Chapter 1,
stops print from adding that extra empty

81
00:04:19,671 --> 00:04:19,995
line.

82
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:21,155
Step 3.

83
00:04:21,175 --> 00:04:25,716
The cafe menu is a small text file, one
item per line.

84
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:29,526
A file opened for reading works in a for
loop.

85
00:04:29,546 --> 00:04:31,770
Line 3 gives one line at a time.

86
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,301
Each line ends with its new line.

87
00:04:34,321 --> 00:04:39,420
Strip, from Chapter 3, removes spaces and
new lines from both ends.

88
00:04:39,467 --> 00:04:42,887
Line 5 prints the name and its number of
letters.

89
00:04:54,247 --> 00:04:54,370
3.

90
00:04:54,390 --> 00:04:58,027
Without strip, the hidden new line would
make it 4.

91
00:04:58,067 --> 00:05:03,378
And Café keeps its accent, because the
file was read as UTF-8.

92
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:04,473
Step 4.

93
00:05:04,493 --> 00:05:07,653
Reading a missing file stops the program.

94
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:11,220
So line 5 checks first, with exists and an
if.

95
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:15,178
Read lines 5 to 8, and the note above the
code.

96
00:05:26,547 --> 00:05:27,062
Line 8.

97
00:05:27,082 --> 00:05:30,730
The file does not exist, so the else block
runs.

98
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:31,792
Step 5.

99
00:05:31,812 --> 00:05:37,412
A relative path, like scores dot csv, is
looked up in the working directory: the

100
00:05:37,432 --> 00:05:39,660
folder you run the program from.

101
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:43,551
Line 4 joins a folder and a file name with
a slash.

102
00:05:43,571 --> 00:05:45,600
It works on every computer.

103
00:05:45,633 --> 00:05:51,257
Lines 5 to 8 print the path, its name and
suffix, its parent folder, and whether it

104
00:05:51,277 --> 00:05:51,733
exists.

105
00:05:51,767 --> 00:05:55,326
There is no reports folder yet, so exists
is False.

106
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:56,494
Breather.

107
00:05:56,514 --> 00:05:59,330
You can write, add, read, and check a
file.

108
00:05:59,367 --> 00:06:00,457
Now, tables.

109
00:06:00,477 --> 00:06:03,789
The cafe's sales live in a CSV file.

110
00:06:08,517 --> 00:06:09,077
Step 6.

111
00:06:09,097 --> 00:06:16,037
A CSV file stores a table as text: one row
per line, with commas between the values.

112
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:19,311
Line 4 opens it with newline equals empty.

113
00:06:19,331 --> 00:06:25,295
The Python documentation asks for this, so
the C S V module controls the line

114
00:06:25,315 --> 00:06:25,917
endings.

115
00:06:25,967 --> 00:06:27,607
Line 5 makes a writer.

116
00:06:27,627 --> 00:06:31,155
Each writerow takes a list, and writes one
row.

117
00:06:31,175 --> 00:06:34,627
The first row is the header: the column
names.

118
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:40,441
Line 10 reads it back as plain text, not
with the C S V module, so it opens the

119
00:06:40,461 --> 00:06:41,468
file normally.

120
00:06:42,087 --> 00:06:42,601
Step 7.

121
00:06:42,621 --> 00:06:46,495
Here are the day's sales: item, quantity
and price.

122
00:06:47,087 --> 00:06:48,656
Line 6 uses DictReader.

123
00:06:48,676 --> 00:06:54,321
It reads the header, then gives each row
as a dictionary, with the column names as

124
00:06:54,341 --> 00:06:54,667
keys.

125
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:58,213
Every value comes back as text, even
twelve.

126
00:06:58,233 --> 00:07:02,550
So line 7 converts with int and float,
from Chapter 4.

127
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:07,867
Line 8 adds each amount to the total, and
line 9 prints the item and its amount.

128
00:07:19,247 --> 00:07:20,357
42.00.

129
00:07:20,377 --> 00:07:24,127
Twelve teas at 3.50.

130
00:07:24,167 --> 00:07:28,652
And line 10 prints the total, 85.00.

131
00:07:29,253 --> 00:07:30,038
Quick check.

132
00:07:30,058 --> 00:07:33,526
Dict reader reads the row: tea, twelve,
three fifty.

133
00:07:33,546 --> 00:07:35,270
What type is the quantity?

134
00:07:46,647 --> 00:07:46,797
C.

135
00:07:46,817 --> 00:07:47,564
A string.

136
00:07:47,584 --> 00:07:50,803
Every value from a C S V file is text.

137
00:07:51,383 --> 00:07:52,804
Back to the mission.

138
00:07:52,824 --> 00:07:57,848
The score file already holds two rounds:
Asha with 4, and Omar with 3.

139
00:07:58,453 --> 00:08:02,713
Lines 4 and 5 hold today's new score:
Lina, with 4.

140
00:08:02,733 --> 00:08:07,323
Lines 7 and 8 append it, in mode A, so the
old rows stay.

141
00:08:07,367 --> 00:08:12,377
Lines 11 to 14 read every row, convert the
score, and print it.

142
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:16,849
Line 15 replaces the best score only when
a score is bigger.

143
00:08:28,213 --> 00:08:28,554
Asha.

144
00:08:28,574 --> 00:08:32,443
Lina's 4 is not bigger than 4, so the
first one stays.

145
00:08:32,467 --> 00:08:33,813
And Lina is saved.

146
00:08:33,833 --> 00:08:36,545
Run it again, and she appears twice.

147
00:08:36,565 --> 00:08:38,747
The file remembers every run!

148
00:08:38,767 --> 00:08:42,423
Now Asha wants the quiz app itself to save
each score.

149
00:08:42,443 --> 00:08:47,120
She builds a save function with an AI
assistant, one stage at a time.

150
00:08:47,717 --> 00:08:50,917
This chapter's AI skill is safe file
handling.

151
00:08:50,937 --> 00:08:54,627
Some file mistakes destroy data with no
error at all.

152
00:08:54,667 --> 00:08:59,117
So test file code on a copy, and read the
file afterwards.

153
00:08:59,167 --> 00:09:01,275
And never paste private data.

154
00:09:01,295 --> 00:09:06,414
A file of real names and scores is
private, so paste a made-up sample.

155
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:08,194
Stage 1: plan.

156
00:09:08,214 --> 00:09:13,140
Asha describes the data to save, and asks
for no code yet.

157
00:09:13,167 --> 00:09:18,830
The AI suggests a C S V file, mode A, and
a header only when the file is new.

158
00:09:18,850 --> 00:09:21,044
That matches what you learned.

159
00:09:21,653 --> 00:09:22,779
Stage 2: write.

160
00:09:22,799 --> 00:09:25,833
Asha writes her own save function first.

161
00:09:25,853 --> 00:09:28,200
Line 7 asks if the file is new.

162
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:31,483
Lines 10 and 11 write the header only
then.

163
00:09:31,533 --> 00:09:37,043
She asks the AI to explain what each call
does to the file, without rewriting it.

164
00:09:37,067 --> 00:09:39,840
She calls it twice, and reads the file.

165
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:41,774
It matches the explanation.

166
00:09:42,387 --> 00:09:43,769
Stage 3: generate.

167
00:09:43,789 --> 00:09:45,483
Asha wants to compare.

168
00:09:45,503 --> 00:09:50,157
She asks the AI for the same function,
from a short request.

169
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:54,699
The AI answers with confidence: it keeps
every score you save.

170
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:58,905
Look at line 6, and remember the three
modes.

171
00:09:58,925 --> 00:10:01,870
Lines 11 and 12 save Asha, then Omar.

172
00:10:13,247 --> 00:10:13,468
No.

173
00:10:13,488 --> 00:10:15,154
Asha's score is gone.

174
00:10:15,174 --> 00:10:20,053
Mode W empties the file on every call, so
only Omar survives.

175
00:10:20,653 --> 00:10:21,696
Stage 4: test.

176
00:10:21,716 --> 00:10:27,163
Asha asks for test cases, each with the
exact text the file should hold.

177
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:32,874
The AI lists three: a new file, two saves,
and an old file with data in it.

178
00:10:33,487 --> 00:10:37,189
She runs all three cases on the AI's
function.

179
00:10:37,209 --> 00:10:38,321
Case 1 passes.

180
00:10:38,341 --> 00:10:42,448
Cases 2 and 3 fail: both lost a row, with
no error.

181
00:10:43,053 --> 00:10:48,313
Asha shows the AI its failing cases, and
asks for the cause, then a fix.

182
00:10:48,333 --> 00:10:54,681
The AI names mode W, and suggests mode A,
with the header only for a new file.

183
00:10:55,287 --> 00:11:00,165
Here is the fix: mode A, and the header
only when the file is new.

184
00:11:00,185 --> 00:11:02,837
The same idea as Asha's own version.

185
00:11:02,867 --> 00:11:05,144
Two saves now keep both rows.

186
00:11:05,753 --> 00:11:08,249
And she runs all three cases again.

187
00:11:08,269 --> 00:11:09,327
All three pass.

188
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:11,250
Stage 5: debug.

189
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:14,940
Asha moves the scores into a data folder.

190
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:18,990
Line 15 saves to data, slash, week dot c s
v.

191
00:11:19,033 --> 00:11:20,171
File not found!

192
00:11:20,191 --> 00:11:23,951
The report points at line 8, inside the
function.

193
00:11:24,553 --> 00:11:28,433
She pastes the error into the chat, and
asks for the cause first.

194
00:11:28,467 --> 00:11:29,608
The AI explains.

195
00:11:29,628 --> 00:11:33,671
Mode A creates a missing file, but not a
missing folder.

196
00:11:33,691 --> 00:11:37,155
The fix makes the folder, only if it is
missing.

197
00:11:37,753 --> 00:11:39,199
Line 7 is the fix.

198
00:11:39,219 --> 00:11:44,655
Then Asha runs every case again, plus a
fourth: a file in a folder.

199
00:11:44,675 --> 00:11:45,795
All four pass.

200
00:11:46,387 --> 00:11:47,711
Stage 6: improve.

201
00:11:47,731 --> 00:11:52,457
Asha asks for one improvement that does
not change the file.

202
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:58,300
The AI says: write the header on every
save, because C S V readers skip repeated

203
00:11:58,320 --> 00:11:58,882
headers.

204
00:11:59,487 --> 00:12:01,554
Here is the shorter version.

205
00:12:01,574 --> 00:12:03,567
Line 10 runs on every call.

206
00:12:03,587 --> 00:12:06,028
Picture the file after two saves.

207
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:17,601
No.

208
00:12:17,621 --> 00:12:21,450
Cases 2 and 3 fail: the header is written
twice.

209
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:27,578
And Dict reader uses only the first row as
the header, so the second header becomes a

210
00:12:27,598 --> 00:12:28,223
data row.

211
00:12:28,243 --> 00:12:29,873
The AI was wrong again.

212
00:12:29,893 --> 00:12:31,380
Asha keeps her check.

213
00:12:31,987 --> 00:12:32,839
Quick check.

214
00:12:32,859 --> 00:12:35,821
An AI's file function runs with no error.

215
00:12:35,841 --> 00:12:38,367
What shows that it keeps every row?

216
00:12:49,747 --> 00:12:49,875
D.

217
00:12:49,895 --> 00:12:52,103
Read the file after two saves.

218
00:12:52,123 --> 00:12:54,777
No error does not mean no data lost.

219
00:12:55,387 --> 00:12:56,008
Breather.

220
00:12:56,028 --> 00:13:00,637
The AI made two confident mistakes, and
Asha's tests caught both.

221
00:13:00,667 --> 00:13:04,252
Next, the four file mistakes every
beginner meets.

222
00:13:08,983 --> 00:13:09,737
Mistake 1.

223
00:13:09,757 --> 00:13:12,597
The file is called scores, with an s.

224
00:13:12,617 --> 00:13:15,303
Line 3 asks for score, without one.

225
00:13:15,333 --> 00:13:21,063
Line 2 printed, then Python stopped: file
not found, with the exact name it looked

226
00:13:21,083 --> 00:13:21,343
for.

227
00:13:21,367 --> 00:13:25,951
Check the spelling, and run the program
from the folder that holds the file.

228
00:13:25,971 --> 00:13:29,222
A relative path is looked up in the
working directory.

229
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:30,679
Now you try.

230
00:13:30,699 --> 00:13:32,804
The cafe log has three lines.

231
00:13:32,824 --> 00:13:35,368
This program meant to add one more.

232
00:13:46,747 --> 00:13:47,009
One.

233
00:13:47,029 --> 00:13:49,126
Mode W emptied the file first.

234
00:13:49,146 --> 00:13:52,867
There was no error, and the three old
lines are gone.

235
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:54,704
The fix: use A to add.

236
00:13:54,724 --> 00:13:58,020
Use W only when you want to start again.

237
00:13:58,620 --> 00:13:59,390
Mistake 3.

238
00:13:59,410 --> 00:14:04,289
An old cash register saved its menu with a
different encoding.

239
00:14:04,309 --> 00:14:09,030
Line 3 reads it as UTF-8, and fails at the
e with an accent.

240
00:14:09,067 --> 00:14:15,192
Find out which encoding saved the file,
read it once with that encoding, and save

241
00:14:15,212 --> 00:14:16,558
it again as UTF-8.

242
00:14:17,153 --> 00:14:17,704
One more.

243
00:14:17,724 --> 00:14:20,049
Remember what Dict reader gives back.

244
00:14:31,413 --> 00:14:35,323
The price is text, and Python cannot add
text to a float.

245
00:14:35,367 --> 00:14:38,286
The fix: convert first, with float.

246
00:14:38,887 --> 00:14:40,187
Here are all four.

247
00:14:40,233 --> 00:14:44,923
File not found: a wrong name, folder or
working directory.

248
00:14:44,967 --> 00:14:48,167
No error, but data gone: W instead of A.

249
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:52,030
Unicode decode error: the file used
another encoding.

250
00:14:52,067 --> 00:14:56,307
And a type error with a string: a C S V
value is text.

251
00:14:56,920 --> 00:14:57,825
Quick check.

252
00:14:57,845 --> 00:15:02,451
A program asks for menu dot text, instead
of menu dot t x t.

253
00:15:02,471 --> 00:15:03,684
What do you see?

254
00:15:15,047 --> 00:15:15,173
A.

255
00:15:15,193 --> 00:15:16,272
File not found.

256
00:15:16,292 --> 00:15:18,910
Python names the file it looked for.

257
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:20,541
Last breather.

258
00:15:20,561 --> 00:15:24,780
You can now read file errors, and the
silent mistake too.

259
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:28,029
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

260
00:15:32,750 --> 00:15:33,132
First.

261
00:15:33,152 --> 00:15:35,681
The score file starts with two scores.

262
00:15:35,701 --> 00:15:38,229
Asha runs the score board three times.

263
00:15:38,249 --> 00:15:42,454
How many score rows, not counting the
header, does it have now?

264
00:15:53,813 --> 00:15:57,801
Then think: how could she stop the same
score being saved twice?

265
00:15:57,821 --> 00:15:59,617
Try it on a copy of the file.

266
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:00,620
Second.

267
00:16:00,640 --> 00:16:04,578
A program crashes halfway through a with
block that writes a file.

268
00:16:04,598 --> 00:16:08,477
What does with still do, and what could be
missing from the file?

269
00:16:19,847 --> 00:16:21,233
Keep your answer.

270
00:16:21,253 --> 00:16:23,219
Chapter 22 builds on it.

271
00:16:23,817 --> 00:16:24,683
Let's recap.

272
00:16:24,703 --> 00:16:27,787
A file keeps data after the program stops.

273
00:16:27,833 --> 00:16:30,993
Write text and read text work on a whole
file.

274
00:16:31,033 --> 00:16:33,616
With open closes the file for you.

275
00:16:33,636 --> 00:16:35,683
W empties, A adds, R reads.

276
00:16:35,733 --> 00:16:39,883
Always write encoding equals utf 8, and
strip each line.

277
00:16:39,933 --> 00:16:44,913
The C S V module writes rows, and reads
them as dictionaries of text.

278
00:16:44,933 --> 00:16:48,372
And test file code on a copy, then read
the file.

279
00:16:48,987 --> 00:16:50,347
Mission complete!

280
00:16:50,367 --> 00:16:53,756
You earned 100 XP and the Archivist badge.

281
00:16:55,983 --> 00:16:57,498
Now it is your turn.

282
00:16:57,518 --> 00:17:00,413
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

283
00:17:00,433 --> 00:17:05,409
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a visit counter that survives

284
00:17:05,429 --> 00:17:06,093
every run.

285
00:17:06,133 --> 00:17:08,996
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

286
00:17:09,016 --> 00:17:13,593
In the last one, you fix an AI's log saver
that loses data.

287
00:17:13,633 --> 00:17:18,424
Work on copies of the data files, so a
mistake never damages the originals.

288
00:17:19,017 --> 00:17:20,382
One problem is left.

289
00:17:20,402 --> 00:17:25,786
If the score file is missing or damaged,
the program still crashes with a long

290
00:17:25,806 --> 00:17:26,687
error report.

291
00:17:26,733 --> 00:17:32,472
In Chapter 22, you will catch errors with
try and except, and show a clear message

292
00:17:32,492 --> 00:17:33,033
instead.

293
00:17:33,067 --> 00:17:34,754
Do the warm-up tasks now.

294
00:17:34,774 --> 00:17:36,667
They take about ten minutes.

295
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:38,655
See you in Chapter 22.
